before:
Run this full SQLite setup and query in a fresh database:
```sql
CREATE TABLE demo_authors (
  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
  name TEXT
);
CREATE TABLE demo_books (
  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
  author_id INTEGER REFERENCES demo_authors(id),
  title TEXT
);
INSERT INTO demo_authors VALUES
  (1, 'Nora'), (2, 'Luis'), (3, 'Imani');
INSERT INTO demo_books VALUES
  (1, 1, 'Sea Path'),
  (2, 1, 'Garden Gate'),
  (3, 2, 'Sky Notes');
SELECT a.name, b.title
FROM demo_authors AS a
INNER JOIN demo_books AS b ON b.author_id = a.id;
```
This returns three matched rows: two for Nora, one for Luis. Imani has no matching book, so INNER JOIN leaves Imani out.

after:
Run this example in a fresh database:
```sql
CREATE TABLE demo_authors(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT);
CREATE TABLE demo_books(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, author_id INTEGER, title TEXT);
INSERT INTO demo_authors VALUES
  (1, 'Nora'), (2, 'Luis'), (3, 'Imani');
INSERT INTO demo_books VALUES
  (1, 1, 'Sea Path'), (2, 1, 'Garden Gate'), (3, 2, 'Sky Notes');
SELECT a.name AS author, b.title
FROM demo_authors AS a
LEFT JOIN demo_books AS b
  ON b.author_id = a.id
ORDER BY a.id, b.title;
```
Nora has two book rows, Luis has one, and Imani has one row with a NULL title.
